          "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nThe Stacks project currently contains two chapters discussing\nmoduli stacks and their properties, see\nModuli Stacks, Section <REF_0001> and\nModuli of Curves, Section <REF_0002>.\nOver time we intend to add more, for example:\n<STRUCT_0002>\n<STRUCT_0003> <MATH_0001>,\ni.e., principally polarized abelian schemes of genus <MATH_0002>,\n<STRUCT_0004> <MATH_0003>, i.e.,\n<MATH_0004>-pointed smooth projective genus <MATH_0005> curves,\n<STRUCT_0005> <MATH_0006>, i.e., smooth projective genus <MATH_0007>-curves\nwith <MATH_0008> pairwise distinct labeled points,\n<STRUCT_0006> <MATH_0009>, i.e.,\nstable <MATH_0010>-pointed nodal projective genus <MATH_0011>-curves,\n<STRUCT_0007> <MATH_0012>, moduli of morphisms\n(with suitable conditions on the stacks <MATH_0013>, <MATH_0014>\nand the base scheme <MATH_0015>),\n<STRUCT_0008> <MATH_0016>, the stack of <MATH_0017>-bundles\nof the geometric Langlands programme (with suitable conditions on the scheme\n<MATH_0018>, the group scheme <MATH_0019>, and the base scheme <MATH_0020>),\n<STRUCT_0009> <MATH_0021>, i.e., the Picard stack associated\nto an algebraic stack over a base scheme (or space).\n<STRUCT_0010>\nMore generally, the Stacks project is somewhat\nlacking in geometrically meaningful examples.\n\n\n",
          "source_text_hash": "sha256:1f78e2a8d4edd9548740d8b285a903f1abd74ea9f2cf89b9d6fb70f5bcd44991",
          "unit_id": "tag:02BL:prose-0001"
        }
